<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Champa</h1><p class="st-meta">192 CE – 1832 CE (approx.) · capital Indrapura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A string of Cham kingdoms along the central Vietnamese coast for some 1,600 years, rich from maritime trade and famous for its red-brick Hindu temples.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Chinese sources record that around 192 a local official in the Han commandery of Rinan rebelled and founded the kingdom of Linyi, usually seen as the origin of Champa. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a>, an Austronesian-speaking people, built a series of polities along the coast from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quang-binh-province">Quang Binh</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phan-thiet">Phan Thiet</a>, using the name Champa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> inscriptions from the 7th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Champa was less a single state than a federation of regions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indrapura-champa">Indrapura</a>, Amaravati, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijaya-champa">Vijaya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauthara">Kauthara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panduranga">Panduranga</a>, each with its own centre. Kings patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> worship, building temple complexes like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/my-son">My Son</a> and Po <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nagar">Nagar</a>, and drew wealth from sea trade in aloeswood, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and ceramics. Champa fought often with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a>, sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> in 1177, and with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ly-dynasty">Dai Viet</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ly-dynasty">Dai Viet</a>'s southward push captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijaya-champa">Vijaya</a> in 1471, leaving only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panduranga">Panduranga</a> in the far south. This rump survived as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> until the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minh-mang">Minh Mang</a> abolished it in 1832. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a>, now largely Muslim, live in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Vietnam</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-cambodia-1953-1970">Cambodia</a> today.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/champa.jpg" alt="Champa" width="320" height="388"><figcaption>Image: Laska666 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linyi_and_Chamic_states_of_the_fifth_century_AD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>192 CE – 1832 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Indrapura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bhadravarman I, Jaya Indravarman IV, Che Bong Nga</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Mahayana Buddhism, later Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cham</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Linyi, Lam Ap, Chiem Thanh, Zhancheng</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1012&amp;focus=champa" aria-label="Champa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7637 1656 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Champa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7957 1912L7769 1191M7957 1912L7771 1192M7957 1912L7846 1933M7957 1912L7930 1822M7957 1912L7930 1822"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0M7771 1192h0M7846 1933h0M7930 1822h0M7930 1822h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7957 1912h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7957 1912h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1012&amp;focus=champa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
